1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,526
Priya's calendar carries four or five client calls a week.

2
00:00:03,526 --> 00:00:07,908
Every one of them ends with a small handful of things somebody agreed to do, and

3
00:00:07,908 --> 00:00:10,482
without fail, some of them evaporate by Friday.

4
00:00:10,482 --> 00:00:11,686
Not from carelessness.

5
00:00:11,686 --> 00:00:14,753
From nobody writing them down in a form anyone re-reads.

6
00:00:14,753 --> 00:00:19,244
Take her weekly Brightwell call as the running example, because this whole section

7
00:00:19,244 --> 00:00:20,230
builds against it.

8
00:00:20,230 --> 00:00:24,556
Twenty five minutes with the account holder, and somewhere in the middle, three

9
00:00:24,556 --> 00:00:29,157
commitments made in passing: she will resend a certificate, he will confirm a figure

10
00:00:29,157 --> 00:00:33,757
with his finance person, and someone, this is the dangerous one, will "look into" an

11
00:00:33,757 --> 00:00:35,345
open question before renewal.

12
00:00:35,345 --> 00:00:39,617
Which of those three is most likely to surface again only when it has become a

13
00:00:39,617 --> 00:00:40,055
problem?

14
00:00:40,055 --> 00:00:41,589
The unowned one, every time.

15
00:00:41,589 --> 00:00:46,079
Meeting notes exist to catch exactly that item, and the reason most people's notes

16
00:00:46,079 --> 00:00:50,241
fail is that writing them well takes twenty minutes nobody has after a call.

17
00:00:50,241 --> 00:00:54,732
Delegating that twenty minutes, without losing the accuracy, is this agent's whole

18
00:00:54,732 --> 00:00:54,951
job.

19
00:00:55,501 --> 00:00:59,888
Read that ceiling line again, because it is the one doing the real work in this

20
00:00:59,888 --> 00:01:00,221
build.

21
00:01:00,221 --> 00:01:04,886
An agent that occasionally invents a due date is worse than no agent at all, because

22
00:01:04,886 --> 00:01:08,051
a wrong list looks exactly as confident as a correct one.

23
00:01:08,051 --> 00:01:11,327
Why does this agent need that ceiling when Agent 1 did not?

24
00:01:11,327 --> 00:01:13,104
Because of how the two can fail.

25
00:01:13,104 --> 00:01:17,769
A triage agent's mistakes are visible by construction: a wrong flag sits in front of

26
00:01:17,769 --> 00:01:19,601
you, asking to be disagreed with.

27
00:01:19,601 --> 00:01:23,988
An extraction agent's natural failure is the plausible gap-fill: the call never

28
00:01:23,988 --> 00:01:28,541
named a date, the list would look tidier with one, so a tidy, reasonable, invented

29
00:01:28,541 --> 00:01:31,540
"by Friday" appears, and nothing about it looks wrong.

30
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:36,094
The ceiling turns that into a bright line you can check against the transcript: if

31
00:01:36,094 --> 00:01:40,369
it was not said out loud, it does not go on the list, and a blank cell is the

32
00:01:40,369 --> 00:01:42,424
correct output, not a failure to fix.

33
00:01:42,424 --> 00:01:46,755
In two lectures, that exact sentence goes into the briefing, and in the trial,

34
00:01:46,755 --> 00:01:51,253
"check every item against your own memory of the call" is checking precisely this

35
00:01:51,253 --> 00:01:52,641
line, not the formatting.

36
00:01:53,191 --> 00:01:58,247
Same method as last section, different kind of question at its core: Agent 1 sorted,

37
00:01:58,247 --> 00:01:59,270
this one distils.

38
00:01:59,270 --> 00:02:02,279
Hold that contrast while the next slide prices it.

39
00:02:02,829 --> 00:02:07,147
That is a real shift in the kind of judgement you are asking for, not just a

40
00:02:07,147 --> 00:02:08,397
different data source.

41
00:02:08,397 --> 00:02:12,999
Section 4.3 is where you write a prompt for extraction instead of classification,

42
00:02:12,999 --> 00:02:15,329
and the two read differently on the page.

43
00:02:15,329 --> 00:02:19,931
The shift matters because the two jobs have different amounts of room to be wrong

44
00:02:19,931 --> 00:02:20,101
in.

45
00:02:20,101 --> 00:02:22,942
A yes or no question can fail in exactly two ways.

46
00:02:22,942 --> 00:02:27,430
Pull three items out of a half hour transcript and there are many more: an item

47
00:02:27,430 --> 00:02:31,748
missed, an item duplicated, the right action pinned on the wrong person, two

48
00:02:31,748 --> 00:02:33,851
people's commitments merged into one.

49
00:02:33,851 --> 00:02:38,623
More freedom, more failure modes, which is why this agent's checking is different in

50
00:02:38,623 --> 00:02:38,907
kind.

51
00:02:38,907 --> 00:02:43,452
You do not skim its output for plausibility, because inventions are plausible by

52
00:02:43,452 --> 00:02:43,850
nature.

53
00:02:43,850 --> 00:02:48,623
You check each line against the call you actually sat in, while your memory of it is

54
00:02:48,623 --> 00:02:49,248
still warm.

55
00:02:49,248 --> 00:02:53,793
That is also why the trial is three transcripts rather than a calendar week: the

56
00:02:53,793 --> 00:02:56,236
unit of evidence here is a call, not a day.

57
00:02:56,786 --> 00:03:01,287
Notice what the list does not contain this time: no routing lecture, because a list

58
00:03:01,287 --> 00:03:05,788
of action items does not fork, and no credential hunt, because of a choice the next

59
00:03:05,788 --> 00:03:07,361
lecture explains and defends.

60
00:03:07,361 --> 00:03:11,754
Agent 2 is the fastest of the four builds, a single sitting end to end, and it is

61
00:03:11,754 --> 00:03:16,200
deliberately sequenced second: same method as Agent 1, smaller mechanical load, so

62
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:17,610
the method is what sticks.

63
00:03:17,610 --> 00:03:21,298
Next lecture starts with the trigger, and it is a pleasant surprise.
